---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **차트에서 어떻게 나가나.** (`/exit`)
 *
 * ── 왜 이 지면인가 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   들어오는 쪽은 이미 여럿 잰다 — /arrival · /climb · /staying-power.
 *   ⚠ **마지막 주에 몇 위였나**는 아무 데도 없었다. 그것이 「떨어졌다」의 뜻을 바꾼다.
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ 시장을 줄세우지 않는다. 양 끝을 나란히 놓고 **왜 다른지**를 같이 적는다.
 *    ⭐ 「좋은 시장/나쁜 시장」이 아니라 **갈아치우는 속도가 다른 것**이라고 화면이 말한다.
 * ⛔ 우리가 고친 것을 지면에서 지우지 않는다. 시즌을 빠뜨려 25,987줄을 잃었던 것을 그대로 적는다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않는다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-exit.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import d from '../../data/wikitip-exit.json';

const 위 = d.korean.byRank[0];
const 아래 = d.korean.byRank[9];

const TITLE = 'How a Korean title leaves a Netflix chart';
const DESC =
  `Everything published about a chart is about getting on it. This is the other end: across `
  + `${d.korean.departures.toLocaleString('en-US')} departures, a Korean title at position 1 is gone the `
  + `next week ${위.endRatePc}% of the time and one at position 10 is gone ${아래.endRatePc}% of the time. `
  + `Measured across ${d.marketCount} markets and ${d.weekCount} weeks.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/exit">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">{d.question}</p>
  <p class="q">
    At position 1 a Korean title is gone the following week <b>{위.endRatePc}%</b> of the time.
    At position 10, <b>{아래.endRatePc}%</b>.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>What share of weeks at each position turned out to be the last</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Position</th><th>Chance it is gone next week</th>
            <th>Weeks spent here</th><th>Departures from here</th><th>Arrivals here</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.korean.byRank.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.rank === 1 || r.rank === 10 ? 'edge' : undefined}>
              <td class="nm">{r.rank}</td>
              <td class="tot">{r.endRatePc}%</td>
              <td>{r.weeksPc}%</td>
              <td>{r.exitPc}%</td>
              <td>{r.entriesPc}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>{d.unit}</b> We found {d.korean.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')} of them, of which{' '}
      {d.korean.departures.toLocaleString('en-US')} ended. {d.whatWasExcluded}
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Arrivals look nothing like departures.</b> {d.korean.byRank[9].entriesPc}% of runs began at
      position 10 and {d.korean.byRank[0].entriesPc}% began at position 1 — getting on a chart is
      spread across the whole list. Leaving it is not. The bottom of a top 10 is not a low position;
      it is the edge of the list.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Korean titles against every title on these charts</h2>
    <p class="body">
      There is an obvious objection to the table above, and it is the right one: if Korean titles
      simply sit near the bottom of charts, bottom-heavy departures would follow automatically and
      mean nothing. {d.whyWeeksColumnIsHere}
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Korean titles</th><th>All titles</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Departures from positions 8–10</td>
            <td class="tot">{d.korean.bottomThreePc}%</td><td>{d.allTitles.bottomThreePc}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Departures from positions 1–3</td>
            <td class="tot">{d.korean.topThreePc}%</td><td>{d.allTitles.topThreePc}%</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Weeks spent in positions 8–10</td>
            <td class="tot">{d.korean.weeksBottomThreePc}%</td><td>{d.allTitles.weeksBottomThreePc}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="q">
      {d.whyTheBaselineIsFree}
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>The last row is the one that decides it.</b> Both groups sit level across the chart, so the
      difference in the first two rows is in how they leave, not where they were. A Korean title here
      is slightly more likely to slide out and slightly less likely to be pushed out while still
      ranking well.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Markets differ, and not by quality</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Departures from the bottom three — highest</th><th></th>
            <th>lowest</th><th></th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.marketsHigh.map((h, i) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{h.국가}</td><td class="tot">{h.아래셋몫}%</td>
              <td class="nm">{[...d.marketsLow].reverse()[i].국가}</td>
              <td class="tot">{[...d.marketsLow].reverse()[i].아래셋몫}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      {d.marketsMeasured} markets have at least {d.marketMinDepartures} measured departures and are
      included; the rest cannot support a distribution and are left out rather than shown thin.
    </p>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>Neither end of this table is better.</b> {d.whyMarketsDiffer}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="src">
    <h2>How this was counted, and what it cannot say</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Source</b> — {d.source}</li>
      <li><b>Rows read</b> — {d.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')} across {d.marketCount} markets and{' '}
        {d.weekCount} weeks.</li>
      <li><b>A gap is measured against that country's own weeks</b>, so a market missing a week is not
        read as a title disappearing and coming back.</li>
      <li>
        <b>A correction we made before publishing.</b> The first version of this count keyed each run
        by country and title, which merged two seasons of one show when both held places in the same
        week — Netflix lists them separately and they are separate runs. It lost 25,987 rows. We
        caught it because the positions we had read came out at 9.28% to 10.56% instead of 10.0%
        each, which is impossible if a chart is read correctly. The collector now refuses to write
        unless that comes out flat, and {d.rowsOverwritten} rows are now unresolved out of{' '}
        {d.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="warn">{d.cannotAnswer}</p>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/arrival">Does it arrive in one country or many</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/climb">Is the week it enters its highest</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/staying-power">How many weeks it lasts</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/returns">Titles that came back</a> · <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/exit" />
</WikiTip>
